  "BEST_WHY": "The surface is actually drawn everywhere it is visible, and it survives being dimmed. In the floor south of the figure (x 150\u2013700, y 700\u2013911), at 4.5\u00d7 exposure the slabs still carry individual identity: slab widths run from ~14px to ~60px on a single course, the joints wander off the grid by a pixel or two rather than ruling straight, and darker patched-in stones sit among lighter ones. Darkness attenuates that surface; it does not replace it. Second: the wall run immediately north of the figure (x 265\u2013430, y 380\u2013392) has a row of short vertical timber pins standing proud of its top edge at irregular spacing \u2014 that is the only place in the deck where the repair carpentry described in the fiction is actually depicted rather than implied. Third, the material boundary at x 503 in the corridor mouth is a genuine butt-joint between two different stone treatments \u2014 pitted tan on the left, flat plated grey-green on the right \u2014 not one texture faded into another.",
  "WORST_WHY": "The construction is wrong, not just thin. The body of water (x 248\u2013343, y 0\u2013191) is an axis-aligned rectangle with 90\u00b0 corners and a one-pixel cut on every side. At its south edge the dark teal ends and brown mud begins on the very next pixel \u2014 no bank, no wet rim, no darker shoreline, no transition tile, and the hex-ripple pattern on the water surface is sliced mid-cell by the cut. Second: the dark brick wall run at x 8\u201356, y 119\u2013143 simply stops in mid-air at x=56. No end stone, no cap, no cross-section \u2014 the wall's last brick abuts open dirt. Third: the orange-and-brown striped bar at x 27\u201334, y 128\u2013167 is an unshaped rectangle of four flat colour bands that starts *inside* the brick wall and continues out the bottom into the dirt, occluding nothing and being occluded by nothing, then terminates in a flat cut with nothing beneath it. Fourth: the crates at x 63\u201397 and the wall cast no contact shadow at all, while the orc at x 213\u2013237 sits on a dark brown oval pad that nothing else in the frame has.",
